§§ `s 122(4) to (6B) -- residing together, child benefit, self-support`

-- (4) A person residing with his spouse is treated as entitled to any child benefit to which his spouse is entitled.
-- src:2629 | (4) A person who is residing with his spouse shall be treated for the purposes of Parts I to V and this Part of this Act as entitled to any child benefit to which his spouse is entitled.
@ref s 122(4) (src:2629)
GIVEN `the person is residing with his spouse` IS A BOOLEAN
      `s 141 the spouse is entitled to child benefit for the week` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 122(4) the person is treated as entitled to the child benefit to which the spouse is entitled` MEANS
    IF `the person is residing with his spouse`
    THEN `s 124(1) holds or else nothing` `s 141 the spouse is entitled to child benefit for the week`
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "A person who is residing with his spouse"

-- (6) "permanently incapable of self-support" if (but only if) both limbs hold.
-- src:2631 | (6) For the purposes of Parts I to V and this Part of this Act a person is “permanently incapable of self-support” if (but only if) he is incapable of supporting himself by reason of physical or mental infirmity and is likely to remain so incapable for the remainder of his life.
@ref s 122(6) (src:2631)
GIVEN `the person is incapable of supporting himself by reason of physical or mental infirmity` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is likely to remain so incapable for the remainder of his life` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 122(6) the person is permanently incapable of self-support` MEANS
    IF `the person is incapable of supporting himself by reason of physical or mental infirmity` AND `the person is likely to remain so incapable for the remainder of his life`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if (but only if)"

-- (6B) The power in (6A) includes power to prescribe an amount that exceeds the arithmetical equivalent of the
-- upper accrual point by not more than 1 pound.
-- src:2633 | (6B) The power conferred by subsection (6A) includes power to prescribe an amount which exceeds by not more than £1 the amount which is the arithmetical equivalent of the upper accrual point.
@ref s 122(6B) (src:2633)
GIVEN `the amount prescribed in pounds` IS A NUMBER
      `the arithmetical equivalent of the upper accrual point in pounds` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 122(6B) the amount prescribed is one the power includes` MEANS
    IF `the amount prescribed in pounds` GREATER THAN `the arithmetical equivalent of the upper accrual point in pounds` AND `the amount prescribed in pounds` AT MOST (`the arithmetical equivalent of the upper accrual point in pounds` PLUS 1)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (silent): "includes power to prescribe an amount which exceeds by not more than £1" does not say that other amounts are outside the power
